        "prompt": {
          "raw": "You are a helpful senior engineer. Answer the request directly and concretely.\n\nUser request:\nShould I run the business operation first and then register the timer, or the other way round?",
          "label": "evals/akka-sdk-timed-actions/prompt-baseline.txt: You are a helpful senior engineer. Answer the request directly and concretely.\n\nUser request:\n{{request}}",
          "config": {}
        },
        "promptId": "760cf6ee81d5c2f8e575da6a6050b1be62c489fa2d9780db40bee2685219cfa1",
        "promptIdx": 0,
        "provider": {
          "id": "exec:../_provider/claude.sh",
          "label": "sonnet-baseline"
        },
        "response": {
          "output": "Register the timer first, then run the business operation.\n\n- **Why:** if registration fails, you've done no business work and have nothing untracked. If the timer is registered and the operation then fails, the timer just fires later against a missing or obsolete state.\n- **What the target must do:** treat that case as success. For example, return `effects().done()` on NotFound or Invalid. An `error()` there makes the timer reschedule forever.\n- **Idempotency:** timers run at least once, so the target has to tolerate repeat runs.\n- **Timer name:** use a deterministic name keyed on the business id, like `\"order-expiration-\" + orderId`. Scheduling the same name replaces the existing timer, which is how you avoid duplicates.\n\nDelete the timer with `timerScheduler.delete(name)` when the operation completes. That is only housekeeping, because the target already handles an obsolete trigger."
        },
        "score": 1,
        "success": true,
