        "prompt": {
          "raw": "You are a helpful senior engineer. Answer the request directly and concretely.\n\nUser request:\nWe renamed a timed action's method and changed its id in a deploy. Any risk?",
          "label": "evals/akka-sdk-timed-actions/prompt-baseline.txt: You are a helpful senior engineer. Answer the request directly and concretely.\n\nUser request:\n{{request}}",
          "config": {}
        },
        "promptId": "760cf6ee81d5c2f8e575da6a6050b1be62c489fa2d9780db40bee2685219cfa1",
        "promptIdx": 0,
        "provider": {
          "id": "exec:../_provider/claude.sh",
          "label": "sonnet-baseline"
        },
        "response": {
          "output": "Yes, there's a real risk. Any timer scheduled before the deploy still points at the old component id and method name. When it fires, the call can't resolve, so it fails and reschedules with backoff (about 3s up to 30s) indefinitely. It stays that way until a compatible deploy, and you get no error at deploy time.\n\n**Fix:**\n1. Check what's pending: `akka services components list-timers <service> -o json`.\n2. Redeploy with a shim for the old id and method. Keep the old `@Component(id = \"<old-id>\")` class with the old method name, and make it delegate to the new logic or return `effects().done()`. A `done()` clears the stale timers.\n3. Keep the shim until `list-timers` shows no timers that reference the old id or method.\n\nNew timers scheduled after the deploy use the new id and method, so they're fine. The old method only has to handle the timers that were already scheduled. Keep it idempotent, because timers are delivered at least once.\n\nIf the old id or method is already gone in production, redeploying the shim recovers it. The timers are retried continuously, so they'll succeed on the next attempt after the shim is live."
        },
        "score": 1,
        "success": true,
